  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 4,
    "lesson_title": "Right-Half-Plane Poles",
    "lesson_core_move": "Identify right-half-plane poles by using their positive real parts to infer growing exponential modes and BIBO instability in causal LTI systems.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Right-Half-Plane Poles",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Right-Half-Plane Poles",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "image-needed",
        "image_slots": [
          {
            "image_slot_id": "section-001",
            "placement": "section",
            "location_placeholder": "[IMAGE-LOCATION:section-001]",
            "description_placeholder": "[IMAGE-DESCRIPTION:section-001]",
            "image_description": "Clean $s$-plane sketch with real axis, imaginary axis, and clearly highlighted right-half-plane region; include one pole at $s=1$ and one pole at $s=-1$. Keep labels minimal and legible. Use the image only to establish the geometric rule for pole regions."
          }
        ],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A right-half-plane pole is identified by its location in the $s$-plane. The right half-plane is the region where the real part of the pole is positive, so the cue to check is the sign of $\\Re\\{s\\}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "image",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": "section-001"
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\text{RHP condition: } \\Re\\{s\\} > 0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For a denominator-root example, use $H(s)=\\frac{1}{s-1}$. The denominator is zero at $s=1$, so the single pole has real part $1$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "H(s)=\\frac{1}{s-1},\\; \\text{pole at } s=1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because $\\Re\\{s\\}=1>0$, this pole is in the right half-plane. In a causal LTI system, that pole contributes a growing exponential mode, so the impulse response is not absolutely integrable and the system is BIBO unstable.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\Re\\{s\\}=1>0\\Rightarrow y(t)\\text{ grows as }e^{t}\\text{ for causal systems}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The classification rule is direct: for a causal LTI system, one or more poles with $\\Re\\{s\\}>0$ means there is at least one growing mode, and the system is BIBO unstable.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Right-Half-Plane Poles",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Identifying a Positive Real Pole",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Classify stability of $H(s)=\\frac{1}{s-4}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A first-order transfer function has poles where the denominator is zero. For this extension of the previous template, set $s-4=0$ to find the pole location.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "s-4=0 \\Rightarrow s=4",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The real part is $\\Re\\{s\\}=4$, so the pole has positive real part.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\Re\\{s\\}=4>0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A positive real part means the pole is in the right-half-plane, which maps to a growing exponential mode in a causal LTI system.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Therefore, $H(s)=\\frac{1}{s-4}$ is unstable in the BIBO sense for a causal LTI interpretation because at least one pole has $\\Re\\{s\\}>0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Right-Half-Plane Poles",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Classifying a Transfer Function with One Right-Half-Plane Pole",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Classify the stability of $H(s)=\\frac{1}{(s-5)(s+2)}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This example shows the same pole test on a two-pole transfer function. The poles come from solving the denominator equation. Start from $ (s-5)(s+2)=0 $.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "(s-5)(s+2)=0 \\Rightarrow s=5 \\text{ or } s=-2",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The pole set is $\\{s=5,\\; s=-2\\}$. At least one pole has a positive real part: $\\Re\\{s\\}=5>0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\Re\\{s\\}=5>0 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\text{RHP pole present}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because one pole is in the right half-plane, this causal LTI transfer function is BIBO unstable even though the second pole at $s=-2$ is stable.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Final classification: unstable.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Right-Half-Plane Poles",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Detecting Right-Half-Plane Poles Among Multiple Poles",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Classify $H(s)=\\frac{10(s+3)}{(s-1)(s+2)(s+5)}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "To scan many poles efficiently, begin by setting each denominator factor to zero so every pole is listed at once.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "(s-1)(s+2)(s+5)=0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "From these factors, the poles are $s=1$, $s=-2$, and $s=-5$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "s=1,\\; s=-2,\\; s=-5",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Check real parts: $\\Re\\{s\\}=1>0$, $\\Re\\{s\\}=-2<0$, and $\\Re\\{s\\}=-5<0$. Only $s=1$ is in the right half-plane.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A single right-half-plane pole is enough to classify the causal LTI transfer function as BIBO unstable; stable left-half-plane poles do not override that result.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Final result: the system is unstable.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Right-Half-Plane Poles",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Recognizing Right-Half-Plane Complex Poles",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Classify $H(s)=\\frac{1}{(s-(2+j))(s-(2-j))}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the same pole-finding cue as before: set each denominator factor to zero, now applied to a complex-conjugate pair.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "s-(2+j)=0, \\; s-(2-j)=0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Those equations give poles at $s=2+j$ and $s=2-j$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\Re\\{2+j\\}=2, \\; \\Re\\{2-j\\}=2",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Since each pole has positive real part, the rule is to classify the causal LTI transfer function as BIBO unstable.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The unstable behavior is growing oscillation, not just oscillation alone, because the shared real part is positive.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Final result: unstable.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
